Instruction:
Write a C++ function that takes a reference to a std::string containing markdown formatted text and returns a std::string containing html formatted text.

Response:

|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\| Here's a C++ function that converts markdown text to HTML:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <map>
#include <regex>

// Define a map to store markdown to HTML conversion rules
std::map<std::string, std::string> markdownToHtmlRules = {
    {"*.*", "<b>$1</b>"},
    {"`.*`", "<i>$1</i>"},
    {"# .*", "<h1>$1</h1>"},
    {"## .*", "<h2>$1</h2>"},
    {"### .*", "<h3>$1</h3>"},
    {"#### .*", "<h4>$1</h4>"},
    {"##### .*", "<h5>$1</h5>"},
    {"###### .*", "<h6>$1</h6>"},
    {"[.*](.*)", "<a href=\"$2\">$1</a>"},
    {"[.*]: .*", "<span class=\"ref\">$1</span>"}
};

/**
 * Converts markdown text to HTML.
 *
 * @param markdown The markdown text to convert.
 * @return The HTML text.
 */
std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html = markdown;
    for (const auto& rule : markdownToHtmlRules) {
        std::regex pattern(rule.first);
        html = std::regex_replace(html, pattern, rule.second);
    }
    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdownText = "# Heading\n* Item 1\n* Item 2";
    std::string htmlText = markdownToHtml(markdownText);
    std::cout << htmlText << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This code defines a function `markdownToHtml` that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text. The function uses a map to store markdown to HTML conversion rules and applies these rules to the input markdown text using regular expressions.

The conversion rules are as follows:

- `*.*` -> `<b>$1</b>` (bold text)
- ``.*`` -> `<i>$1</i>` (italic text)
- `# .*` -> `<h1>$1</h1>` (heading 1)
- `## .*` -> `<h2>$1</h2>` (heading 2)
- `### .*` -> `<h3>$1</h3>` (heading 3)
- `#### .*` -> `<h4>$1</h4>` (heading 4)
- `##### .*` -> `<h5>$1</h5>` (heading 5)
- `###### .*` -> `<h6>$1</h6>` (heading 6)
- `[.*](.*)` -> `<a href=\"$2\">$1</a>` (link)
- `[.*]: .*` -> `<span class=\"ref\">$1</span>` (reference)

The `main` function demonstrates how to use the `markdownToHtml` function to convert markdown text to HTML.

